  code { font-family:ui-monospace,monospace; background:var(--code-bg); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:4px; padding:.1em .3em; font-size:.88em; }
  textarea { width:100%; min-height:70px; background:var(--code-bg); color:var(--text); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:6px; padding:.6rem; font-size:.92rem; }
  details { margin-top:.5rem; color:var(--muted); }
</style>
<script>
function pick(btn, ok) {
  const q = btn.closest('.q');
  if (q.classList.contains('done')) return;
  q.classList.add('done');
  btn.classList.add(ok ? 'right' : 'wrong');
  if (!ok) q.querySelectorAll('.opt').forEach(o => { if (o.dataset.ok === '1') o.classList.add('right'); });
}
</script>
</head>
<body><div class="wrap">
<div class="meta">Drill 0010 · pairs with lesson 0010</div>
<h1>Request-Id Routing</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. Player 2 asks the baker a question. What key tells the host the resulting stream belongs to player 2?</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The NPC index — the baker knows who asked</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">The request id returned by npc.ask()/LlmClient::submit(), recorded in the router's inflight_ map as {playerId, npcIndex}</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The TCP socket the deltas arrive on</button>
<div class="why">Deltas arrive on the host's LLM queue, not a socket. The unique submit id is claimed at ask-time: inflight_[id] = {playerId, npcIndex}.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. In the host loop, <code>routeDelta()</code> returning false means…</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The delta was corrupt and dropped</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">The delta belongs to the host's own conversation; the pre-existing solo code handles it, unchanged</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The target player disconnected</button>
<div class="why">Consume-or-pass: new consumer gets first refusal, old code path can't tell multiplayer exists. Disconnected players are handled inside routeDelta (sendToPlayer fails silently), which still returns true.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. Two players message the same NPC at nearly the same time. What guarantees neither reply is lost or interleaved?</h3>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">The router queues lines per-NPC and only submits when !npc.waiting(); the second line waits for the first reply to resolve</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">LlmClient's single worker thread is enough on its own</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The second message is rejected with an error</button>
<div class="why">The worker serializes HTTP calls, but Npc tracks only ONE pendingId_ — a second ask() would clobber the first. The per-NPC queue is what protects Npc's single-pending invariant.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Why do <code>NpcSpeechBubble</code>/<code>NpcMoodUpdate</code> broadcast to everyone while the error case goes only to the asker?</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">Broadcasting errors would be too much bandwidth</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">Speech and mood are world state every player can see; a failed LLM call is a private problem only the asker can act on (retry)</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">Errors contain secrets</button>
<div class="why">Audience follows meaning: shared consequences broadcast, private outcomes route to their owner.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. Written recall:</h3>
<p>A player disconnects while their reply is streaming. Walk through what happens to (a) the stream, (b) the NPC's history, (c) the next player who asks that NPC.</p>
<textarea placeholder="(a) ..."></textarea>
<details><summary>Reference answer</summary>(a) routeDelta/routeReply still fire, but sendToPlayer() finds no live connection and returns false — the payload evaporates. (b) npc.onReplyArrived() still runs, so history/mood update consistently (the world doesn't fork). (c) The router pumps that NPC's queue as usual; the NPC is free and answers normally — one client's death never wedges shared state.</details></div>
</div></body></html>
